window.BEA_AUDIO_MANIFEST={"schema":1,"model":"hexgrad/Kokoro-82M","clips":{"A balanced diet includes varied foods.":{"bm_george":"audio/bm_george/040e0db40d8647f230cd78f8.mp3","bf_emma":"audio/bf_emma/040e0db40d8647f230cd78f8.mp3"},"Cities often experience higher temperatures than surrounding rural areas, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island effect. Dark surfaces such as asphalt absorb solar energy during the day and release heat slowly at night. Buildings can also restrict airflow, while vehicles and air-conditioning equipment produce additional heat. Researchers have investigated ways to reduce this effect. Street trees offer shade and can cool their surroundings through evaporation. Reflective roofs may reduce heat absorption, although their performance varies with climate and maintenance. One small study found that tree-lined streets were cooler in the afternoon than nearby streets without trees. The researchers cautioned that differences in traffic and building height might also have influenced the measurements. They recommended collecting data across different seasons before estimating city-wide benefits.":{"bm_george":"audio/bm_george/7bd8a01f4d3a471406f0e4ef.mp3","bf_emma":"audio/bf_emma/7bd8a01f4d3a471406f0e4ef.mp3"},"Hello! What is your name? My name is William. I am from Myanmar. Nice to meet you, William. Nice to meet you too.":{"bm_george":"audio/bm_george/c522103d17b97dfe7b95d26d.mp3","bf_emma":"audio/bf_emma/c522103d17b97dfe7b95d26d.mp3"},"The field team measured street temperatures at two in the afternoon. The shaded avenue averaged twenty-nine degrees, whereas the nearby exposed road averaged thirty-three degrees.":{"bm_george":"audio/bm_george/70deb4a42a16fda8c0b8bf4a.mp3","bf_emma":"audio/bf_emma/70deb4a42a16fda8c0b8bf4a.mp3"},"accommodation":{"bm_george":"audio/bm_george/a05744e1bf9b659d8b0cc85d.mp3","bf_emma":"audio/bf_emma/a05744e1bf9b659d8b0cc85d.mp3"}}};
